WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.344 --> 00:00:01.177
[metal clinks]

00:00:01.177 --> 00:00:02.232
[metal clinks]

00:00:02.232 --> 00:00:03.523
[lid clicks]

00:00:03.523 --> 00:00:04.510
[metals clink]

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.951
Tara Blume: Every Foley
stage probably has a machete.

00:00:06.951 --> 00:00:08.950
I'm not sure that every Foley artist

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.925
actually has a machete at their house...

00:00:10.925 --> 00:00:11.816
[laughs]

00:00:11.816 --> 00:00:16.312
[lid thuds]

00:00:16.312 --> 00:00:17.503
Joe Avella: So, to start off,

00:00:17.503 --> 00:00:19.120
can you please introduce yourself?

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.571
Tara: My name Is Tara Blume,
and I'm a Foley artist.

00:00:22.571 --> 00:00:23.861
[boards break]

00:00:23.861 --> 00:00:25.341
[rag squishes]

00:00:25.341 --> 00:00:27.106
[wood scratching]

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:35.340
Normally in a Foley session,
they'll roll the beeps,

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.700
and then the picture will follow,

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.166
and then we will perform
in sync with the picture.

00:00:39.166 --> 00:00:41.723
[plastic crinkles]
[metal clatters]

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.602
Tara: I picked "Evil Dead II,"

00:00:52.602 --> 00:00:54.050
the chainsaw build.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:55.617
[fabric rustles]

00:00:55.617 --> 00:00:56.450
[metal clanks]

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.380
Ash: Groovy.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.440
Tara: Mostly because I
figured that I could replicate

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.350
what's in that video.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.270
Like, not very precise,

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:07.290
and, like, the sound
quality was not very good.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.300
First off, I muted the sound,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.740
and then watched it a
couple times silently,

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.440
'cause what I was trying
to do is match picture.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.180
That's normally how we get a project,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.230
there is no sound.

00:01:21.265 --> 00:01:23.510
[metal clangs]

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.200
I knew I wanted to use the machete

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.640
just to give a blade
sound for the chainsaw.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.960
When I was first performing it,

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:34.959
I was imagining closing this on itself,

00:01:34.959 --> 00:01:38.063
so, like, we're getting,
like, an insert sound of it.

00:01:38.063 --> 00:01:39.050
But it wasn't really,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:39.883
sorry, sorry!
[wheel screeching]

00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:41.050
Joe: No, that's fine, that's OK. [laughs]

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.270
Tara: It wasn't really
giving me an insert sound,

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.940
so as I was performing it,

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.667
I ended up just going with picture.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.793
[metals clink]

00:01:50.793 --> 00:01:53.443
So, you can hear how the cavity of it

00:01:53.443 --> 00:01:55.780
is giving you, like, an insert sound,

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.990
and then just the blade
ricocheting at the same time

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:00.347
was giving me the blade sound.

00:02:00.347 --> 00:02:03.650
I'm not actually looking at
picture while performing.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.816
That was the biggest challenge to me.

00:02:07.711 --> 00:02:08.544
[latch clicks]

00:02:08.544 --> 00:02:09.377
[chain rattles]

00:02:09.377 --> 00:02:10.210
[door squeaks]

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:11.043
[door bangs]

00:02:11.043 --> 00:02:11.876
[latch clicks]

00:02:11.876 --> 00:02:12.709
[chain rattles]

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:13.542
[door squeaks]

00:02:13.542 --> 00:02:14.375
[door bangs]

00:02:14.375 --> 00:02:17.360
The gate outside my door,
I took from my neighbor

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.340
'cause they left it out for trash.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:23.162
I was gonna change it into,
like, a succulent wall. [laughs]

00:02:23.162 --> 00:02:24.842
[hinges squeak]

00:02:24.842 --> 00:02:26.412
[gate thuds]

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:27.805
[hinges squeak]

00:02:27.805 --> 00:02:29.666
[gate thuds]

00:02:29.666 --> 00:02:33.310
The other think is a plantar fasciitis,

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:36.103
like, a foot fascia roller.

00:02:36.103 --> 00:02:38.670
[wood squeaks]
[rolling taps]

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.803
Like, the first wood squeak that happens.

00:02:40.803 --> 00:02:42.735
Like, I have always noticed
the squeak on that thing.

00:02:42.735 --> 00:02:43.570
[wood squeaks]
[rolling taps]

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.909
The only chain I have
is a bike-lock chain.

00:02:45.909 --> 00:02:46.742
[chain rattles]

00:02:46.742 --> 00:02:49.660
Like, I know how certain
things sound in my kitchen,

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.130
and, I don't know, yeah,

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.263
I think it's just because
of the nature of the job.

00:02:56.308 --> 00:02:57.358
[lightbulb chain clicks]

00:02:57.358 --> 00:02:58.430
[metals clank]

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.150
I don't have real lightbulbs at my house.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.010
They're all energy-saving,

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.300
and they don't sound like lightbulbs, so.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.033
[metals clink]

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.270
I ended up putting, like, an old lantern

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.568
that I have and a chain on it,

00:03:14.568 --> 00:03:16.790
but if I had a lightbulb

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.690
it would have probably
sounded better. [laughs]

00:03:21.266 --> 00:03:23.540
[metals clatter]

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.124
[metals thud]

00:03:25.124 --> 00:03:26.231
[drill scratches]

00:03:26.231 --> 00:03:27.742
[metal thuds]

00:03:27.742 --> 00:03:29.200
[metal thuds]
[metal clatters]

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.117
[metals clank]

00:03:30.117 --> 00:03:31.020
[metals scratch]

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.180
I just grabbed a bunch of
different metal objects

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.348
that I have just to create
different metal rattles.

00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:39.015
[metal rattles]

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:44.440
In the visual, it's, like,
running on screws and stuff,

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.607
so you can, like, see a bunch
of different metal rattling.

00:03:47.607 --> 00:03:48.840
[metal rattles]

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.490
There's, like, brackets
that get thrown down,

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:52.528
one after the other.

00:03:52.528 --> 00:03:54.130
[metals thud]

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:57.513
The closest thing I
have to that are spurs.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:02.080
The screwdriver we
typically wouldn't cover,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.410
but I just figured that
I should do something,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.110
'cause this is a Foley exercise,

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:10.539
so I just used a screw on the machete.

00:04:10.539 --> 00:04:11.680
[screw scraping]

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.030
Every Foley stage probably
has a machete or two.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.485
I'm not sure that every
Foley artist actually

00:04:17.485 --> 00:04:20.260
has a machete at their house...[laughs]

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:21.280
but I do.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.787
I also have a full-sized ax.

00:04:26.013 --> 00:04:29.946
And this crowbar, which is extra big.

00:04:32.059 --> 00:04:33.596
[lid clicks]

00:04:33.596 --> 00:04:35.000
[metals squeak]
[lid clicks]

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.730
So, I just wanted to provide,
like, a metal squeak,

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:39.990
and then, like, a closure sound.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.490
And I'm holding it with my foot

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.770
because I also didn't want to edit it

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:47.206
and I wanted it to happen just
like the visual happens, so.

00:04:47.206 --> 00:04:48.040
[lid clicks]
[wheel squeaks]

00:04:49.927 --> 00:04:50.925
[chains rattle]

00:04:50.925 --> 00:04:51.843
[fabric tightens]

00:04:51.843 --> 00:04:52.792
[chains rattle]

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:53.625
[fabric tightens]

00:04:53.625 --> 00:04:56.370
You have to, like, really
be precise with your action,

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.130
so that became kind of difficult

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:00.470
just because it had to be right in sync

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.236
and I wasn't really watching the picture.

00:05:02.236 --> 00:05:04.030
[fabric rustles]

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:07.450
So, I actually built that
prop, the leather straps,

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:10.510
for "Westworld," for
the samurai character,

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:14.720
which you can't hear very
much of, but it's in there.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.100
That take was pretty hard to do,

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.680
because I was fighting
the level of the metal

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.460
on the table to the leather.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:24.246
The metal was overtaking
the leather sound.

00:05:24.246 --> 00:05:25.600
[chain rattle]
[fabric rustles]

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.317
I added a piece of cloth underneath

00:05:28.317 --> 00:05:30.400
the piece of metal I was grabbing.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.505
The leather harness that tightened...

00:05:33.505 --> 00:05:35.444
[fabric tightens]

00:05:35.444 --> 00:05:37.310
[fabric rustles]

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:40.390
I would have done, like,
a rope-grab kinda sound,

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:41.890
and then I would have also done

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.631
a leather creak on top of that,

00:05:43.631 --> 00:05:44.600
[fabric tightens]

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:46.410
but having to do it simultaneously

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:47.652
was definitely a challenge.

00:05:49.707 --> 00:05:50.602
[latch clicks]

00:05:50.602 --> 00:05:51.640
[metal thuds]

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.090
The Crock-Pot was right in front of me,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.370
so my mind immediately was like,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:57.640
what can I stuff in there

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.040
that's gonna give me, like, a sound

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.822
that something is going into something?

00:06:00.822 --> 00:06:04.348
[metal thuds]

00:06:04.348 --> 00:06:06.820
Also I was recording in this space.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.550
I was worried about it, 'cause
it's obviously super live,

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:12.530
the ceilings are really
high and the floor is wood,

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:13.940
and it actually worked out.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.110
Honestly, when I chose this room,

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.505
it was because of this surface.

00:06:17.505 --> 00:06:19.080
[wood thuds]

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.310
Use this as, like, a
base of sound...sorry.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.351
Joe: No, it's OK [laughs]

00:06:26.351 --> 00:06:27.184
[chainsaw cocks]

00:06:27.184 --> 00:06:28.017
[metal clang]

00:06:28.017 --> 00:06:29.217
[chainsaw cocks]

00:06:29.217 --> 00:06:30.050
[metal clang]

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:30.960
[chainsaw starting

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.252
That is an actual handsaw.

00:06:34.252 --> 00:06:36.735
[handsaw clicks]

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.000
That was just giving me, like, a large,

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.770
plastic kind of
chainsaw-body kind of thing.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.507
The other thing is actually
an airsoft handgun.

00:06:46.507 --> 00:06:47.340
[gun cocks]

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.990
I obviously needed the sound of

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:53.468
the chainsaw starting,
like the cable pull.

00:06:53.468 --> 00:06:55.951
[wire scratches]

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.720
I would have used something

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:01.053
way different on the Foley stage.

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:03.510
I probably would have used a chain

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.791
up against, like, a big body
of plastic or something.

00:07:06.791 --> 00:07:08.320
But this worked pretty well!

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.677
I just used the body of a bucket.

00:07:10.677 --> 00:07:11.510
[wire scratches]

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:14.240
And it's actually almost
comical because of the visual,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.550
because it doesn't work.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.620
You hold it, it, like, dies out,

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:19.836
and she, like, expels air.

00:07:21.708 --> 00:07:23.649
[chainsaw starts]

00:07:23.649 --> 00:07:25.640
[chainsaw revs]

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:26.780
That's actually a sound effect.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:30.300
Foley of a chainsaw would be
the handling of a chainsaw.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.390
The grab of the cable,

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:36.530
and then running of a
chainsaw is an effect sound,

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:37.430
not Foley.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:41.400
So for, like, a gun, a
gunshot would be an effect,

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.008
and then the movement of
the gun would be Foley.

00:07:45.929 --> 00:07:48.103
[gun whooshes]

00:07:48.103 --> 00:07:50.153
[whooshing]

00:07:50.153 --> 00:07:54.902
This is actually a garden,
you know, trellis stick.

00:07:54.902 --> 00:07:57.319
[stick whooshes]

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.240
Those whooshing sounds are
always made by a doily.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.410
A doily is just a thin stick,

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:07.450
when thrusted through the air

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:10.617
creates a high-pitched "whoosh" sound.

00:08:10.617 --> 00:08:12.690
[doily whooshes]

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:14.880
The different shapes of different sticks

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.530
make different, lower whooshes
or higher-pitched whooshes,

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:19.505
kind of interesting.

00:08:22.286 --> 00:08:23.119
[wood squeaks]
[rolling taps]

00:08:23.119 --> 00:08:23.952
[chain rattles]

00:08:23.952 --> 00:08:25.230
[hinges squeak]
[gate thuds]

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.035
[metals clank]

00:08:28.035 --> 00:08:29.700
[metals thud]

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.717
[metal scratches]

00:08:31.717 --> 00:08:32.550
[objects clank]

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:33.383
[lid clicks]

00:08:33.383 --> 00:08:35.601
[metal clicks]

00:08:35.601 --> 00:08:37.297
[fabric rustles]

00:08:37.297 --> 00:08:38.612
[metals click]

00:08:38.612 --> 00:08:40.834
[metals clank]

00:08:40.834 --> 00:08:43.031
[wire scratches]

00:08:43.031 --> 00:08:47.603
[handsaw cocks]
[chainsaw idles]

00:08:47.603 --> 00:08:50.903
[chainsaw roars]
[clang]

00:08:50.903 --> 00:08:53.486
[handsaw cocks]
[chainsaw idles]

00:08:55.508 --> 00:08:57.852
[doily whooshes]

00:08:57.852 --> 00:09:00.185
[gun cocks]

00:09:04.155 --> 00:09:05.171
Ash: Groovy.

00:09:05.171 --> 00:09:08.780
Joe: Why didn't you just do
the sound effects on your own

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.540
and then in edit try
and, like, match it up?

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:14.530
Tara: Because I'm an artist,
not an editor. [laughs]

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:16.820
So it was way easier for
me to just perform it

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:18.609
right in sync than for me to edit it.

00:09:18.609 --> 00:09:19.556
[laughs]

